{"context":"The EKA AI Skill Pack (ADR-022) ships as the `skills/` zone of this repository. Its official installation path is manual copying: users copy skill folders into their agent's configuration directory (`~/.config/opencode/skills/`, `~/.claude/skills/`, `~/.agents/skills/`) and the command files into the commands directory. Three problems follow:\n\n1. **Multi-step, error-prone install** — the user must know the exact per-agent target locations and copy each folder.\n2. **Drift** — the pack evolves (skills are added, command bodies change), and installed copies in agent configurations go stale; there is no update mechanism and no version record at the target.\n3. **No official distribution path** — an \"official skill pack\" with only a copy-paste installation is not distributable; there is no discoverable, verifiable, idempotent install.\n\nThe CLI is the canonical executable form of the ecosystem (Naming and Terminology §7) and the natural distribution vehicle: it already ships via release binaries (`install.sh` / `install.ps1`). ADR-022 Decision 1 states the pack itself adds **no** new CLI commands — installation commands are the deliberate exception: they serve the pack's *distribution*, not its runtime behavior, and the pack continues to function with manual copying alone.\n\nThe embedding question is decisive: the `eka` binary is standalone and portable (ADR-009; the CLI docs' \"standalone, portable binary\"). If the pack is a compile-time resource of the binary, installation works everywhere the binary works (including release-binary installs), and the binary version and the pack version travel together — the drift problem disappears at the source. The repository already has the embedding precedent: `skeletonembed.go` (a root-level package exposing the module-root `skeleton/` directory as an `embed.FS`) existed until ADR-020 removed it because `eka init` became identity-only — its purpose, not the mechanism, died. This milestone revives the mechanism for the skill pack.","decision":"1. **`eka install skills` and `eka install commands` are new CLI commands.** They copy the embedded skill pack into an agent configuration directory. The parent family (`eka install`) mirrors the single-operation interaction model (context header + report + summary); subcommands mirror `eka sync pull/push` and `eka integrity check`. The commands are **distribution convenience, not runtime surface**: they never touch the EKA workspace or the canonical store, and they are not required for the pack to function (manual copy remains valid — ADR-022 Decision 1 stands, amended by this exception).\n\n2. **The pack is embedded in the binary.** A root-level package (`skillpackembed.go`, `package skillpackembed`, `//go:embed skills` — the skeletonembed precedent) makes the pack a compile-time resource. Consequences: installation never depends on the pack being present on disk; release-binary users install without a checkout; **the binary version and the pack version are coupled by construction** (the pack version is read from the embedded `skills/manifest.yaml` at runtime, so the CLI and the manifest cannot drift). The `--link` (symlink) installation mode is **dropped**: with an embedded source there is no source path to symlink to. Developers working from a checkout re-run `eka install` — it is idempotent.\n\n3. **Targets mirror the agent ecosystems' configuration locations** (resolved against the user's home directory): `opencode` (`~/.config/opencode/{skills,commands}`), `claude` (`~/.claude/{skills,commands}`), `agents` (`~/.agents/skills` — skills only; the agents standard has no commands directory, refused). Without `--target`, the command **auto-detects** the targets whose configuration directory exists; when none exists it refuses deterministically (exit 1, the environment-state refusal class, with the `--target`/`--dir` hint). `--target all` installs to every supported target. An unknown `--target` value or a `--target`+`--dir` combination is a usage error (exit 2). `--dir` installs into an explicit directory (project-scoped installs, tests) and bypasses detection.\n\n4. **Install semantics are deterministic and idempotent.** Skills install as self-contained folders (each skill directory with `SKILL.md` plus its resources — the `eka-knowledge-authoring` templates travel with the skill); commands install as the two command files. A state marker (`.ekapack.json`: pack, kind, version — no timestamps) is written into each target and drives the re-install report: the same version reports `unchanged (refresh)`, a different version reports `updated`. Copies overwrite silently (refresh semantics). `--dry-run` prints the plan without writing anything — dry-run and execution share the same plan and cannot diverge (the `eka init --dry-run` contract). Output is deterministic: identical input produces identical output.\n\n5. **The version contract moves into the binary.** The pack's `requiresEka` (manifest) is the minimum CLI version the pack content is written against; with embedding, the installed pack's version equals the installing binary's pack version by construction, so installs cannot install a pack newer than the CLI that reads it — the exact drift the manifest's `requiresEka` guarded against is now structurally impossible on the install path (manual copies can still drift; re-running `eka install` fixes them).","consequences":"- **Positive.** One-command installation with target detection, version-aware updates, and deterministic output; the pack becomes genuinely distributable through the existing release-binary pipeline; binary and pack versions travel together; the install path is testable (hermetic `--dir` tests) and smoke-testable.\n- **Negative.** The binary grows by the pack's size (approximately 50 markdown/JSON files — negligible); pack updates require a binary update (the flip side of version coupling; release cadence becomes the pack's delivery cadence); the root-level embed package revives a mechanism ADR-020 removed (justified: different purpose — distribution, not scaffolding).\n- **Risks.** Agent ecosystems may change their configuration locations (mitigated: `--dir` for explicit installs, `--target` list is a small table in one file); users may expect `eka install` to update the binary itself (out of scope — `install.sh`/`install.ps1` remain the binary update path); `--link`-style live-editing workflows (repo checkout → agent config) lose their symlink convenience (mitigated: idempotent re-install).\n\n### Future Extensibility (not implemented)\n\n- **`eka install` growth** — additional targets (new agent ecosystems), project-scoped install UX, or an `--update`-style flag, over the same embedded source.\n- **Pack extraction** — `eka install --export \u003cdir\u003e` writing the full pack (skills + commands + docs) for offline/air-gapped distribution.\n- **Version negotiation** — install-time warning when the embedded pack's `requiresEka` exceeds the running binary's version (structural today, defensive later).\n\n### References\n\n- [ADR-022](adr-022-ai-skill-pack.md) — the AI Skill Pack this command distributes; Decision 1 (no new CLI commands) amended by the distribution exception\n- [ADR-009](adr-009-knowledge-runtime-architecture.md) — the standalone portable binary the embed travels in\n- [ADR-020](adr-020-repository-bootstrap-namespace-reconciliation.md) — removed the embedded skeleton (identity-only init); the embed mechanism is revived here for a different purpose\n- The embed — [`../../skillpackembed.go`](../../skillpackembed.go) (root-level package, the `skeletonembed` precedent)\n- The command — [`../../cmd/install.go`](../../cmd/install.go), [`../../cmd/install_test.go`](../../cmd/install_test.go)\n- The pack — [`../../skills/manifest.yaml`](../../skills/manifest.yaml) (version source), [`../../skills/docs/installation.md`](../../skills/docs/installation.md) (installation documentation)\n- ADR index: [`../adr-summary.md`](../adr-summary.md)","alternativesConsidered":"- **Repo-checkout source** (`eka install` reads `./skills` from disk) — rejected: fails for release-binary installs, the most common case.\n- **Release-asset fetch** (skills tarball as a release asset, the `install.sh` pattern) — rejected for v1: extra distribution infrastructure (assets, checksums, version resolution) for the same outcome embedding gives for free.\n- **Install script in the pack** (`skills/scripts/install.sh`) — rejected: not discoverable via `eka`, no version coupling, no target detection UX.\n- **Symlink (`--link`) installation** — rejected: no source path with an embedded pack; would require a repo-checkout source (alternative 1).\n- **Standalone `eka uninstall` / `eka update` commands** — rejected for v1: re-running `eka install` is the update; uninstall is deleting the installed folders (documented, not commanded)."}